<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1896</h1><p class="ref-lede">1896 in world history: 28 events, 17 births, 19 deaths, 3 states founded and 5 that came to an end. 878 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a><a href="/years/1893">1893</a><a href="/years/1894">1894</a><a href="/years/1895">1895</a><a href="/years/1896" aria-current="page">1896</a><a href="/years/1897">1897</a><a href="/years/1898">1898</a><a href="/years/1899">1899</a><a href="/years/1900">1900</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/paris" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paris.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-1">1 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Puccini's 'La bohème' premieres in Turin</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Conducted by the young Arturo Toscanini at the Teatro Regio, Puccini's opera of struggling artists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> received a cool critical welcome. Audiences loved it, and it became one of the most performed operas.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menelik-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Ethiopia defeats Italy at the Battle of Adwa</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>'s army routed an Italian invasion force in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. The victory forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to recognise Ethiopian independence and became a lasting symbol of African resistance.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adwa">Battle of Adwa</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Henri Becquerel discovers radioactivity</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Becquerel found that uranium salts left in a drawer had fogged a photographic plate without any sunlight. He had discovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a>, soon investigated by Marie and Pierre Curie.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/athens" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">First modern Olympic Games open in Athens</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-i-of-greece">George I of Greece</a> opened the first modern Olympics at the Panathenaic Stadium. Revived by Pierre de Coubertin, the Games drew some 240 athletes from 14 nations.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Plessy v. Ferguson upholds 'separate but equal'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The US Supreme Court ruled that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/racial-segregation">racial segregation</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> railways did not violate the Constitution. The decision underpinned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws">Jim Crow laws</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brown-v-board-of-education">Brown v. Board of Education</a> in 1954.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-26">26 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Nicholas II is crowned Tsar of Russia</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> and Alexandra were crowned in the Kremlin's Dormition Cathedral. Days later more than a thousand people died in a crush at the celebrations on Khodynka Field, a grim omen for the reign.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Guglielmo Marconi applies for a wireless telegraphy patent</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The young Italian inventor filed his application in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> for a system of telegraphy without wires. Within five years his signals had crossed the Atlantic.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-16">16 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Gold is found on Bonanza Creek in the Klondike</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">George Carmack, Skookum Jim and Dawson Charlie struck gold on a tributary of the Klondike River in the Yukon. When news reached the south in 1897, some 100,000 people set out for the goldfields.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-17">17 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Bridget Driscoll becomes Britain's first pedestrian killed by a car</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Bridget Driscoll was struck by a demonstration car in the grounds of the Crystal Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The coroner hoped such a thing would never happen again.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Alfred Nobel dies in San Remo</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a>, the Swedish chemist who invented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dynamite">dynamite</a>, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. His will left most of his fortune to fund prizes, first awarded on the anniversary of his death in 1901.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zanzibar-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-zanzibar-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zanzibar-war">Anglo-Zanzibar War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Anglo-Zanzibar War was a brief conflict on 27 August 1896 between Britain and the Sultanate of Zanzibar.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/coup-of-rucunshu">Coup of Rucunshu</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Coup of Rucunshu was a succession struggle at the Rwandan court in late 1896.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-addis-ababa">Treaty of Addis Ababa</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Treaty of Addis Ababa was signed in 1896 between Ethiopia and Italy after the Ethiopian victory at the Battle of Adwa.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-adibo">Battle of Adibo</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Adibo was fought in 1896 near Yendi, in what is now northern Ghana, between German colonial forces advancing from Togo and the army of the Dagomba kingdom of Dagbon.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Battle</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shangi">Battle of Shangi</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Battle of Shangi was fought in 1896 in south-western Rwanda, near Lake Kivu, when a Rwandan army attacked a post set up by the Congo Free State.</p></div></li></ol></section>
